WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.021
While Netflix is good for an afternoon of
binge-watching your favorite TV shows, it

00:00:04.021 --> 00:00:05.990
has a great movie selection, too.

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:10.160
Even though the titles Netflix offers change
from time to time, they consistently offer

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.190
up some really great movies, from older classics
to more recent treasures.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.680
Do yourself a favor and add to your queue
these hidden gems on Netflix.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:19.860
Kagemusha (1980)

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:25.280
Akira Kurosawa's Kagemusha stands as one of
the legendary director’s finest works.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.119
Amazingly, Kurosawa could not secure financing
for the film in Japan, prompting Kurosawa

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:34.870
fans George Lucas and Francis Ford Coppola
to get 20th Century Fox to produce it.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:39.960
The sweeping epic won the Palme d'Or at Cannes
and reignited Kurosawa's career.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.460
The Last Unicorn (1982)

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:46.460
Yes, The Last Unicorn is an animated children's
movie, but don’t lump this haunting and

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.750
beautiful tale in with the usual Disney fare.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.570
For starters, the gorgeously unique animation
style might be familiar to some viewers, as

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:58.290
the core members of its animation team would
go on to form Japan’s Studio Ghibli.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:02.969
The tale is brought to life by a stellar voice
cast that includes Christopher Lee, Jeff Bridges

00:01:02.969 --> 00:01:04.070
and Mia Farrow.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:08.850
And the enchanting score is provided by '70s
soft rockers America.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:12.830
“At the last unicorn.”

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.540
The Thin Blue Line (1988)

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:20.090
Long before Making a Murderer, there was The
Thin Blue Line.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:24.460
Former private detective Errol Morris directed
this chilling documentary with the intention

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:28.570
of bringing more attention to the case of
Randall Adams, a man sentenced to death for

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.369
the murder of a police officer.

00:01:30.369 --> 00:01:35.460
Morris' work paid off when his film, and the
many discrepancies in the evidence and testimony

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.979
it uncovered, prompted officials to reopen
the case, eventually exonerating Adams and

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:41.940
overturning his conviction.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.570
Things We Lost in the Fire (2007)

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:48.670
Benicio del Toro portrays Jerry, a struggling
heroin addict trying to reconnect with life

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:49.670
and love.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:54.299
He's joined by Audrey (played by Halle Berry),
who faces her own uphill climb as a recently

00:01:54.299 --> 00:01:55.869
widowed mother of two.

00:01:55.869 --> 00:02:00.950
This drama avoids the formulaic and focuses
on the poignant—and with stunning performances

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.340
from both Berry and del Toro, it's a must-watch.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.520
Dope (2015)

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.950
This coming-of-age dramedy puts self-described
"geek" Malcolm (Shameik Moore) and his friends

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:15.879
in a difficult position when his application
to Harvard is jeopardized by a drug deal.

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:20.489
Moore gives an outstanding performance, and
director Rick Famuyiwa pulls us along on a

00:02:20.489 --> 00:02:24.480
wild ride with a smart story that's downright
hilarious at times.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.290
“You probably got like one of those photogenic
brains”

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:28.739
“You mean photographic memory?”

00:02:28.739 --> 00:02:30.389
“What did I just say?”

00:02:30.389 --> 00:02:32.559
“I mean yeah, you said it.

00:02:32.559 --> 00:02:34.099
Yeah, reiterating.”

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:36.359
Beasts of No Nation (2015)

00:02:36.359 --> 00:02:41.230
Netflix made its first major foray into original
films when it acquired Beasts of No Nation.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:46.099
Directed by True Detective vet Cary Fukunaga,
it tells the harrowing, powerful tale of a

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:51.889
West African child caught in a civil war and
recruited to a rebel militia as a child soldier.

00:02:51.889 --> 00:02:58.189
Idris Elba gives a commanding—and terrifying—performance
as the militia leader who orders his soldiers

00:02:58.189 --> 00:03:00.739
to perform ever more horrific acts.

00:03:00.739 --> 00:03:02.260
The Fundamentals of Caring (2016)

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:07.319
Another winner from Netflix's stable of original
films, The Fundamentals of Caring takes the

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:10.829
"road movie" formula and gives it an offbeat
twist.

00:03:10.829 --> 00:03:18.019
“Ahhhhh, help me, help me, Ahhhhh!”

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:23.349
Paul Rudd and Craig Roberts team up as, respectively,
a caretaker and a teen afflicted with muscular

00:03:23.349 --> 00:03:28.749
dystrophy out to explore the country and themselves
(while picking up a hitchhiking Selena Gomez

00:03:28.749 --> 00:03:29.870
along the way).

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:34.209
Writer-director Rob Burnett's deft touch with
the material makes for an enjoyably diverting

00:03:34.209 --> 00:03:35.510
journey.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:36.689
Thanks for watching!

00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:40.479
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:40.479 --> 00:03:43.439
And leave us a comment to let us the rest
of us know about your favorite little-known

00:03:43.439 --> 00:03:44.719
Netflix gems.

